  "answer_text": "In Massachusetts, the maximum amount you can collect for unemployment depends on your prior earnings, whether you have dependents, and the number of weeks you qualify for. \n\nThe breakdown of the maximum limits includes the following:\n\n### 1. Maximum Weekly Benefit Amount\n* **Base Maximum:** **$1,105 per week**. \n* **With Dependents:** If you have dependent children and are their main source of support, you may be eligible for a dependency allowance of **$25 per week per child**. This dependency allowance is added on top of your weekly benefit but is capped at 50% of your base weekly benefit amount.\n\n### 2. Maximum Duration (Number of Weeks)\n* **30 Weeks:** While the standard maximum duration in Massachusetts is typically 26 weeks, state law dictates that if the 12-month average unemployment rate in any of the state's designated metropolitan areas rises above 5.1%, the maximum duration increases to **30 weeks** statewide. This trigger was activated in April 2025 and remains in effect. \n\n### 3. Maximum Total Benefit Amount\n* **Without Dependents:** **$33,150** ($1,105 per week for 30 weeks).\n* **With Dependents:** If you qualify for the maximum weekly benefit and receive a dependency allowance for children, your total payout over 30 weeks will be higher. \n\n### How Your Personal Benefit is Determined\nTo receive the absolute maximum amount, you must have earned a high enough wage during your \"base period\" (the primary look-back period of your employment history, usually the first four of the last five completed calendar quarters). Massachusetts typically calculates your weekly benefit at roughly **50% of your average weekly wage** during your two highest-earning quarters, up to that $1,105 cap.",
